 We compare how many tests each tool needs to detect each previously known bug. For \toolname{}, a test is one generated program run once: we run tests generated from the invisible-speculation specification (\S\ref{sec:example-contract}) on all three AMuLeT builds at AMuLeT's own configurations\cutsug{, drawing the cache state and the branch-stall timing at random for each test (\S\ref{sec:speculation-directives})}: InvisiSpec and SpecLFB run the first 4{,}000 tests of one corpus, and CleanupSpec all 291{,}860 tests of two corpora and three random re-concretizations of the first. For AMuLeT, we run its own fuzzer 32 times with its own settings and count test cases, one program on one input. Both sides use the same estimate of tests per detection, $(N{+}1)/(K{+}1)$ for $K$ detections in $N$ tests; error bars in Figs.~\ref{fig:firstdetv2} and~\ref{fig:timedet} are 95\% Clopper-Pearson intervals on each side's detection rate. \toolname{} needs $94\times$ to $1{,}655\times$ fewer tests for every previously known bug except A-UV5, rare for both tools, for which it needs about $3.7\times$ fewer (Fig.~\ref{fig:firstdetv2}). Two factors contribute, and we do not separate them: every \toolname{} test contains a transmitter whose operand is in the protection set, and the harness forces each test's mispredictions (\S\ref{sec:speculation-directives}).
 
 \subsection{Timing And CPU Usage}\label{sec:timing}
-We measure cost as wall-clock time on equal numbers of cores. Each \toolname{} test is one single-threaded gem5 run at AMuLeT's configuration for the defense, so its wall time equals its CPU time. Generating a test takes an estimated 0.93 CPU-seconds and running it 1.3 to 1.7, by defense; both tools' per-test costs are measured on the same idle node, each keeping about 16 hardware threads busy. Counting generation, on the same number of cores \toolname{} detects A-UV1, A-UV4 and A-UV6 $4.9$--$53\times$ faster than AMuLeT, while AMuLeT detects A-UV3 about $1.2\times$ faster and the rare A-UV5 about $31\times$ faster (Fig.~\ref{fig:timedet}). Generation is a one-time cost, and the generated tests are reused when a defense is patched; AMuLeT generates programs inside its fuzzing loop, so its time per detection already includes generation.
